<worksheet>
<title>Factoring Trinomials - Part 2</title>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section you will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Factor a trinomial of the form <m>ax^2 + bx +c</m>
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    By adding a number in front of the <m>x^2</m>-term that is not 1 adds another step to factoring. We will combine what we learned in the last section with factoring 
    by grouping from the previous section to complete the factoring. First, try to factor out a GCF, this could turn your factoring problem into one from the last section
    and make your problem simpler. IF you cannot factor out a GCF. Then multiply <m>a</m> and <m>c</m>. Call the product the key number. Next, look for two numbers that
    add to <m>b</m> and multiply to the key number. Then break up <m>b</m> using the two numbers. This will give you four terms. Complete the factoring by grouping. 
</p>
<example>
    <title>Factor</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Factor the expression <m>2x^2-3x-20</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
           <m>(x-4)(2x+5)</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            First multiply 2 and -20 to get the key number of -40. Now look for two numbers that add to -3 and multiply to -40. The two numbers are -8 and 5. Rewrite the expression
            as <m>2x^2 - 8x + 5x -20</m>. Factor by grouping. <m>2x^2 - 8x + 5x -20 = 2x(x-4) + 5(x-4) = (x-4)(2x+5)</m>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example>
    <title>Factor</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Factor the expression <m>63x^2-8x-16</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
           <m>(7x-4)(9x+4)</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            First multiply 63 and -16 to get the key number of -1008. Now look for two numbers that add to -8 and multiply to -1008. The two numbers are -36 and 28. Rewrite the expression
            as <m>63x^2 - 36x + 28x -16</m>. Factor by grouping. <m>63x^2 - 36x + 28x -16 = 9x(7x-4) + 4(7x-4) = (7x-4)(9x+4)</m>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example>
    <title>Factor</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Factor the expression <m>5x^2+38x+21</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
           <m>(5x+3)(x+7)</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            First multiply 5 and 21 to get the key number of 105. Now look for two numbers that add to 38 and multiply to 105. The two numbers are 3 and 35. Rewrite the expression
            as <m>5x^2 +3x + 35x +21</m>. Factor by grouping. <m>5x^2 +3x + 35x +21 = x(5x+3) + 7(5x+3) = (5x+3)(x+7)</m>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<p>
    <alert>Try it Now!</alert>
</p>
